{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CfbCU5Wtp8LAnEVMBfBs9","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I wrote the candidate, `running_lead_surprise_path`, and it passed the self-check (`OK`). On the smoke fit it scores about 347 nats worse than the current best model (ELPD-LOO −2725.7 against −2378.9), so it is unlikely to win. It is an exploratory mechanism, not a contender.\n\n**Hypothesis:** People keep a running tally of heads minus tails as they read a sequence left to right. At each flip they judge how surprising the current lead is, given how many flips they have seen so far: a lead of two after two flips is much more striking than a lead of two after eight. A sequence's sense of randomness is the average of that surprise along the whole tally path. Each person compares it with their own ideal amount of surprise, and on some trials they guess at a personal lapse rate.\n\n**Why this mechanism:**\n- **Running tally:** It answers this slot's brief, because the path of the tally matters, not just the final count.\n- **Opening streaks:** Since surprise is scaled by how many flips have been seen, a streak at the start counts against a sequence much more than the same streak at the end. That targets the critique's finding that people penalise opening streaks more than the current best model predicts.\n- **Not a re-proposal of `tally_drift_ideal`:** That pruned model judged overall wandering of the tally against a personal ideal. This one scales each prefix's lead by how many flips have been read, which is what produces the start-versus-end difference.\n\n**Model:** Each sequence gets one feature, the log of the average squared lead divided by flips seen. Each person has their own ideal value, there is one shared sensitivity to distance from that ideal, and each person has their own lapse rate.\n\n**Smoke-fit warnings:**\n- R-hat could not be computed, because the check samples only one chain. Convergence h